fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1019", description: some(value: "GENERATE an entity representation in ViewHead for the native-carrier values, instead of ViewHead::Opaque plus a side-channel equality. A Value::OpRef is {op: Symbol, dict: Option<RequirementHandle>, named: Option<Symbol>} — a PURE VALUE whose op and named halves are plain symbols. It has structure; the view simply does not present it. The same holds for Value::FactRef. Presenting that structure AS AN ENTITY (a reflect-form head with named children) makes equality, goal_fingerprint keying, discrim indexing and unification all fall out of the machinery that already exists for every other structural form — nothing special-cased. WHY THE CURRENT STOPGAP IS THE WRONG SHAPE, and it is mine: WI-1014 follow-up added Value::native_carrier_eq plus a (ViewHead::Opaque, ViewHead::Opaque) arm in views_structurally_equal, because an OpRef MEASURED as not equal to ITSELF. That fixes equality and NOTHING ELSE — two distinct OpRefs still share one goal_fingerprint, because the head stays payload-free — so eq and the key now DISAGREE, which is a worse state to reason about than both being wrong together. It is also a SECOND equality path that has to be kept in sync with the structural one by hand, the kind of duplication WI-486 collapsed when it made views_structurally_equal the single structural compare. THE SHAPE TO REUSE is already in term_view.rs: wrapped_expr_head / wrapped_expr_keys / wrapped_expr_child present an Expr variant as Fn{<reserved reflect qname>, named: [...]} off ONE shape table, so head arity and named_keys cannot drift apart (the WI-814 discipline). An OpRef would read as Fn{anthill.prelude.Function.op_ref, named: [op: Ref(op), named: Option<Ref>]} or similar. DECIDE: (1) the functor must be a RESERVED kernel-vocab name so a generated head cannot collide with a user entity of the same name — and note WI-1014 just made wrapped_expr_head LOUD on an unresolved reflect qname, so a new form needs its symbol declared by the standard load or it will panic rather than degrade; (2) does the reflect surface need a matching TERM twin, or is the view representation enough? WI-425 says a view must agree with its twin; if no twin exists there is nothing to disagree with, but then a value_to_term of an OpRef has no target either; (3) SCOPE — which carriers get one. OpRef and FactRef carry pure structural content. Closure / Map / Cell / Stream / Substitution / Requirement are ARENA HANDLES whose content mutates behind a slot, so a content entity would be unstable; for those the representation would have to be the slot IDENTITY, or they stay Opaque with a documented by-nature reason (which the Value::Relation doc already states: a native carrier is not structural data). Do NOT assume the whole group answers the same way — MEASURE each with the two probes that found this: is it equal to itself, and do two distinct ones share a key? Both answered wrong for OpRef AND FactRef. ON DELIVERY retire native_carrier_eq and the (Opaque, Opaque) arm so there is ONE equality again, and MEASURE the blast radius the way WI-1014 did: is_cacheable's Opaque exclusion starts admitting these goals, discrim starts indexing heads that previously could not be stored at all (insert_walk PANICS on an Opaque head), and value_fact_dedup_key stops degrading for them — which is the point, but it means facts that were never deduped start being deduped. SUPERSEDES the specific WI-1018 (Value::OpRef must not be Opaque), which this is the mechanism for."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-05T16:00:50Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1019", author: "user", content: "SCOPE DECIDED (user): add REFLECT ENTITIES for the operation reference and for FactRef, rather than a new ViewHead variant or a payload-bearing opaque. reflect declarations live in stdlib/anthill/reflect/reflect.anthill. TWO THINGS THE IMPLEMENTER MUST NOT MISS. (1) THE dict TRAP for OperationRef. The entity must NOT present only op and named. dict is the dispatch environment captured at MINT in the eta-site frame (WI-420); two OpRefs with the SAME op and named but DIFFERENT dicts dispatch under different requirement environments and are not the same value. An entity view that drops dict makes them structurally EQUAL and share a key — a FALSE POSITIVE, the dangerous direction, because this feeds value_fact_dedup where merging DROPS A FACT (WI-815). Note the current stopgap native_carrier_eq is STRICTER than that: it compares dict by slot (RequirementHandle's doc: 'Identity is (arena, raw)'). So a naive entity representation would be a REGRESSION against the thing it replaces. Options: (a) present the Value::Requirement as the dict child and leave it Opaque for now — the key then degrades via is_opaque_free, which is safe, but equality needs a Requirement arm by slot identity or an OpRef WITH a dict stops being equal to itself again; (b) give Requirement its own representation too — it is genuinely structural, (functor, [sub-dicts]) readable via handle.functor() / arity() / project(k), and the runtime already names it anthill.realization.runtime.Dictionary. (b) removes the Opaque entirely and is the recursive, complete answer. Do NOT encode dict as a slot-id scalar: that leaks an arena index into a structural view and two arenas can collide. (2) FactRef IS ALREADY DECLARED, as 'sort FactRef = ?' (reflect.anthill:35) — an ABSTRACT, CONSTRUCTOR-LESS sort, deliberately: 'application code can pass it to persistence mutation operations but cannot construct, print, or persist it as a durable identifier.' So this must be a VIEW-level representation, NOT an anthill-surface constructor; adding a surface constructor would contradict the declared contract. That is precisely the distinction I got wrong twice when picking wi815_head's subject: ViewHead is internal machinery, so keying on a value's identity exposes nothing to anthill, while the sort's 'opaque' is a statement about the SURFACE. Both can hold at once. FactRef's payload is a two-variant enum (Resident{rule, mirror} / External{owner, key}) — decide whether the view keys on the whole payload or on a session-scoped identity. The functor of any new form must be declared by the standard load, since WI-1014 made wrapped_expr_head PANIC on an unresolved reflect qname rather than degrade.", at: "2026-08-05T13:35:06Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1019", author: "user", content: "WHERE THE RULE GOES, decided (user): NOT a new proposal — both types already have a document that owns them. OperationRef extends docs/proposals/library/006-reflection-record-schemas.md (a reflection record schema is exactly what an operation-reference representation is). FactRef extends docs/proposals/library/005-stored-references.md, which already defines FactRef, its opacity contract, and why it is not reconstructible from a visible Term — so its view representation is a rule about that same type and must be written consistent with what 005 already says, not invented beside it. Correcting my own suggestion in passing: I proposed writing 'proposal 005' as a NEW document without looking, and 005 was already taken by the very type under discussion. READ 005 AND 006 IN FULL FIRST, then write the rule, then implement against it — the standing rule this whole ticket keeps re-learning (WI-1014 shipped a wrong tuple shape derived from sibling code while docs/kernel-language.md stated it three times). The design content to carry in: the dict trap (an OperationRef record dropping dict is LOOSER than the native_carrier_eq stopgap it replaces, in the direction that merges facts), and the view-level vs surface-level opacity distinction (ViewHead is internal machinery, so keying on identity exposes nothing to anthill, while 'sort FactRef = ?' is a statement about the SURFACE — both hold at once). SEPARATE FINDING, not this ticket: enum StoredRef is declared in anthill.reflect but is a PERSISTENCE pair, consumed only by stdlib/anthill/persistence/store.anthill, and the reflect header names only Term and FactRef as the abstract types it means to define. Namespace placement, worth its own item.", at: "2026-08-05T15:04:24Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1019", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED, and the ticket's own grouping was backwards — which the USER caught, not me. THE PLACEMENT DECISION WAS BUILT ON A MISSING FACT. Both prior scope notes assumed the operation reference had no declared home and needed a new reflect record under 006. It already had one: sort OpRef (accessors op, dict) and sort Dictionary (impl, arity, sub, resolveOp, ops) in stdlib/anthill/realization/runtime.anthill, both constructor-less, builtins registered since WI-577, owned by docs/design/requirement-dictionaries.md §2. That is the SAME mistake the previous note corrected in itself ('I proposed writing proposal 005 as a NEW document without looking, and 005 was already taken'), recurring one step later: I read 005 and 006 in full as instructed and still did not look for a THIRD document, because the two named ones felt like the whole question. The rule that would have caught it is not 'read the named docs' but 'find the doc that owns the TYPE'. 006 is about loader-emitted rows with declared field schemas; an OpRef is not a row.\n\nTHE GROUPING WAS INVERTED, BOTH WAYS. The ticket said OpRef and FactRef 'carry pure structural content' and put Requirement with the arena handles 'whose content mutates behind a slot'. Measured: a requirement slot is {functor, requirements, refcount} and is NEVER mutated after alloc — only refcount moves — and requirement-dictionaries.md §2.1 already said so ('immutable resolved values — no identity/mutation hazard (unlike Cell), so a structural reflect view is a pure view'). Meanwhile FactRef belongs with the handles. The USER supplied the discriminator after I had written a worse one: I framed it as 'reference into mutable storage vs resolved value', and the reply was that an OpRef is a reference too — it holds the operation's NAME. The real line is what the reference is SPELLED IN. An OpRef names its target with a Symbol, which has a Value carrier and denotes the same op to every reader; a FactRef locates its row by a private slot index (RuleId/RowKey) with NO Value carrier. One has a shape to present, the other only an identity to hold. Same word, opposite sides.\n\nI ALSO GOT THE STABILITY ARGUMENT BACKWARDS AND CAUGHT IT ONLY BY MEASURING. I was about to claim FactRef could not be content-keyed because RuleIds are reused after retract. They are not: retract sets a tombstone and entries are only appended (kb/mod.rs:428 states it outright). The arena is the one that reuses slots — RequirementArena has a free_list. So the WI-1014 stopgap compared dicts by the UNSTABLE half. Two facts, both opposite to my assumption, one command apart.\n\nA REAL DEFECT IN THE RETIRED STOPGAP, found by reading its own doc against its code: native_carrier_eq compared dictionaries with ha.raw() == hb.raw() while RequirementHandle's doc says 'Identity is (arena, raw)'. It dropped the arena half, so two dicts from different interpreter arenas sharing a raw compared EQUAL — a false positive, the fact-merging direction (WI-815), and reachable because a fresh interpreter per call is the normal pattern. Content comparison has no arena half to drop. Pinned by dictionaries_from_two_arenas_sharing_a_raw_are_not_equal.\n\nSHIPPED. Value::OpRef views as Functor{runtime.OpRef, 0, N} with named op (always) / dict / named (conditional on Some — the Expr::Proof precedent, so no Option wrapper is synthesized); Value::Requirement as Functor{runtime.Dictionary, arity, 1} with named impl and the sub-dicts positional, which is exactly its impl/arity/sub accessor set. Field keys resolve through DECLARED ACCESSOR qnames, not bare short names: reflect_field_key's lookup_symbol finds a short name only if something happened to intern it, and 'named' is spelled nowhere in the stdlib — it would have panicked on the first OpRef carrying one. New ViewItem::Owned(Value) for children the parent COMPUTES rather than stores; every reader pairs it with the borrowed arm so nothing downstream can tell them apart. The (Opaque, Opaque) arm is RE-FOUNDED, not retired as the ticket asked: with OpRef and Requirement now shaped, what remains is the law for carriers that genuinely have no shape — identity for equality, a deliberately coarse key that is_opaque_free already guards. native_carrier_eq -> opaque_carrier_eq, answering only for FactRef via FactRef::locates_same_row (a named method, not a PartialEq derive — Eq-ness is a claim 005 makes about the SURFACE).\n\nTWO GAPS CLOSED IN PASSING. runtime.anthill's header claimed 'the accessor ops expose everything the value holds' and had no accessor for named — declared it plus a builtin, driven through Interpreter::call on a real resolveOp-minted ref. And 005 said FactRef is 'not an Eq value'; the user had that clause removed, since it was the surface statement that made 'not equal to itself' look like policy.\n\nCONTROLS MEASURED, not predicted — and two of my predictions were wrong, so the comments now state the measurements. Five back-outs, each biting: OpRef head->Opaque fails 4 tests; Requirement head->Opaque fails 3; drop dict from the shape fails exactly two_oprefs_differing_only_in_their_dictionary_are_distinct; drop named fails exactly the_named_spec_op_half_is_part_of_the_identity; delete the (Opaque, Opaque) arm fails ONLY the FactRef unit test — which is the proof the two halves are independent. Dropping the OpRef.named registration fails the new accessor test while resolve_op_remembers_the_named_spec_op stays green, which is why the value-level test could never have caught that gap. wi815_head keeps FactRef as its opaque subject and its doc comment turns out to have been right all along. Full workspace: 4164 passed, 0 failed.", at: "2026-08-05T16:00:44Z")
